<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">White Monuments of Vladimir and Suzdal</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1992">1992</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The White Monuments of Vladimir and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suzdal">Suzdal</a> are eight medieval churches and buildings of white limestone in and around Vladimir and Suzdal in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, inscribed together as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 1992. Built mainly in the twelfth and thirteenth centuries, they include the Dormition and St Demetrius cathedrals and the Golden Gate in Vladimir, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-the-intercession-on-the-nerl">Church of the Intercession on the Nerl</a> and the Nativity Cathedral of Suzdal.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/white-monuments-of-vladimir-and-suzdal.jpg" alt="White Monuments of Vladimir and Suzdal" width="320" height="448" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Trasprd · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:White_Monuments_of_Vladimir_and_Suzdal_Montage.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
